We present the minimal extension to the simplest seesaw-I framework capable of generating at least one light neutral current neutrino oscillation via the spontaneous breaking of neutral current neutrino seesaw symmetry. This represents the minimal requirement for neutrino mass seesaw symmetry. In this model, a unique feature is the emergence of an additional neutral current, giving rise to a "super-oscillation" phenomenon, which we exploit to detect and identify the seesaw symmetry breaking scale ($\Lambda_{
u}$). We establish the condition necessary for this symmetry breaking to occur naturally. Assuming this natural breaking, we demonstrate that the mass of the new heavy Higgs boson ($M_{2}$) is directly determined by $\Lambda_{
u}$, while the mixing angle between the light and heavy neutral Higgs states is likewise fixed by $\Lambda_{
u}$. Consequently, the model possesses a single free parameter, $\Lambda_{
u}$. We anticipate that future high-luminosity high-energy $pp$ colliders will be able to measure this angle and determine $\Lambda_{
u}$. Furthermore, we prove that the mass of the lightest supersee-saw particle ($M_{S1}$) is also governed by $\Lambda_{
u}$. Based on this relationship, we outline a strategy to select a viable $\Lambda_{
u}$ value such that $M_{S1}$ aligns with the desired dark matter density ($\Omega_{DM}$). Within this feasible parameter range, we provide evidence that the see-saw mechanism, dark matter, baryon asymmetry, and electric charge quantization can all be explained. We also present data supporting the stability of the Higgs vacuum within this region. Regarding detection, we detail how the "super-oscillation" phenomenon can be utilized at future $pp$ colliders to identify the neutrino seesaw symmetry breaking scale ($\Lambda_{
u} \sim 10^{2}-10^{4}$ TeV). Finally, we propose that once $\Lambda_{
u}$ is determined, the dark matter particle's mass ($M_{S1}$) and mixing angle ($\xi$) can be measured at future $\gamma p$ and $pp$ colliders.
